  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Focus on Freedom - FoRB - Middle-East &amp; Asia</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This programme addresses the growing pressure on Freedom of Religion and Belief (FoRB) in Iraq, Syria, Indonesia, Pakistan and the Philippines (Mindanao). In all five contexts, increasing polarisation, politicisation of religion and shrinking civic space create environments in which religious minorities, women and young people face intersecting forms of exclusion &#x2013; not only because of what they believe, but also because of who they are. Discrimination, social marginalisation and limited access to decision-making spaces undermine inclusive citizenship and social cohesion.

Building on Mensen met een Missie&#x2019;s longstanding experience in peacebuilding and religion-sensitive programming, the programme is locally led and conflict-sensitive. It supports trusted in-country civil society organisations &#x2013; including faith-based actors, women- and youth-led groups and minority advocates &#x2013; to address structural drivers of exclusion from within their own societies. Local partners foster dialogue within religious communities, build bridges between communities, and advocate for more inclusive policies and practices at national, regional and international levels.

The core strategy follows three interconnected pathways:

Intrafaith: working within religious communities to challenge exclusionary interpretations, harmful norms and practices that restrict the rights and agency of minorities, women and youth;

Interfaith: strengthening dialogue, cooperation and mediation between religious communities to reduce tensions, counter &#x201c;othering&#x201d; narratives and prevent violent extremism;

Extrafaith: engaging formal and informal authorities to improve implementation of FoRB-related laws, promote non-discriminatory education, protect FoRB defenders, and translate national commitments into local practice.

Across these pathways, the programme invests in women of faith, interfaith youth and FoRB ambassadors as long-term connectors and mediators; supports legal and psychosocial assistance for at-risk minorities and human rights defenders; and promotes youth-led digital peacebuilding and responsible media engagement to counter online hate and polarisation.

Through multi-year, partner-driven capacity strengthening, participatory grantmaking and locally anchored dialogue structures, the programme aims to expand safe space for conversation, reinforce advocacy capacities and contribute to durable improvements in Freedom of Religion and Belief in Iraq, Syria, Indonesia, Pakistan and the Philippines.</narrative>
  </description>
